Topic 1: What is Recursion?
Recursion means a function calls itself to solve a smaller version of the same problem.
Imagine standing between two mirrors: you see a repeated image, but in programming, the function must have a stopping condition so it doesn't keep calling itself forever.
Example:
def count_down(n):    if n == 0:       # Base case: stop        return    print(n)    count_down(n - 1)  # Recursive call



If we call count_down(3), it prints 3, then 2, then 1, and stops at 0.





Topic 3: The Call Stack
Look at our code:
def count_down(n):    if n == 0:        return    print(n)    count_down(n - 1)



When we call count_down(3), Python pauses each unfinished call while the next one runs.
Python's call stack
count_down(3) — waiting for the next call

count_down(2) — waiting for the next call

count_down(1) — waiting for the next call

count_down(0) — base case reached; returns


Once count_down(0) returns, Python finishes the paused calls in reverse order: count_down(1), then count_down(2), then count_down(3).
This is why the call stack follows LIFO: Last In, First Out.
